WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.372
Embeddings and indexes are sensitive infrastructure in a private RAG system.

00:00:04.372 --> 00:00:08.643
An embedding is a mathematical representation used to support similarity search,

00:00:08.643 --> 00:00:11.361
but it is derived from source content.

00:00:11.361 --> 00:00:15.245
When the source material is sensitive, the embedding, nearby metadata,

00:00:15.245 --> 00:00:19.904
and index records deserve careful treatment as part of the data architecture.

00:00:19.904 --> 00:00:24.953
Vector stores may hold embeddings, chunk text, document IDs, tenant labels, permission tags,

00:00:24.953 --> 00:00:26.894
source references, and lifecycle metadata.

00:00:26.894 --> 00:00:29.224
They are not just performance caches.

00:00:29.224 --> 00:00:33.884
They are systems that influence which evidence reaches the model and which

00:00:33.884 --> 00:00:35.826
users can discover source-derived material.

00:00:35.826 --> 00:00:40.097
Collection and namespace design should follow data sensitivity, ownership, tenant boundaries,

00:00:40.097 --> 00:00:41.262
and query patterns.

00:00:41.262 --> 00:00:45.922
Mixing unrelated tenants or sensitivity levels into one broad index can make

00:00:45.922 --> 00:00:48.252
permission enforcement and lifecycle management harder.

00:00:48.252 --> 00:00:50.582
Separate collections are not always required,

00:00:50.582 --> 00:00:54.465
but the design should make access rules clear and testable.

00:00:54.465 --> 00:00:56.407
Metadata filters should be deliberate.

00:00:56.407 --> 00:01:00.290
Tenant identifiers, classification labels, lifecycle status, source type, effective dates,

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.008
and permission fields often become retrieval controls.

00:01:03.008 --> 00:01:05.726
If those fields are incomplete or inconsistent,

00:01:05.726 --> 00:01:09.609
the retrieval layer may select content that is stale, irrelevant,

00:01:09.609 --> 00:01:11.551
or inappropriate for the workflow.

00:01:11.551 --> 00:01:13.881
Index refresh and deletion behavior matter.

00:01:13.881 --> 00:01:17.764
Documents change, permissions move, projects close, and old guidance expires.

00:01:17.764 --> 00:01:22.424
The architecture should define how indexes are rebuilt, updated, backed up, restored,

00:01:22.424 --> 00:01:23.201
and purged.

00:01:23.201 --> 00:01:27.861
Administrative access to indexes should be protected because index operators may influence

00:01:27.861 --> 00:01:30.191
retrieval behavior and source-derived data availability.

00:01:30.191 --> 00:01:35.627
Teams should document what each index contains, who owns it, how it is refreshed,

00:01:35.627 --> 00:01:39.122
and what operational signals show that it is healthy.

00:01:39.122 --> 00:01:43.782
That record helps reviewers treat the vector store as a managed data

00:01:43.782 --> 00:01:46.500
system rather than a hidden implementation detail.
